  "articleBody": "When you're making your home more accessible for someone using a walker, it's easy to overlook a few key things that can really impact usability. One common mistake is just assuming a standard interior doorway will be fine. While walkers are generally narrower than wheelchairs, they still need adequate space for safe, comfortable passage, often 32-36 inches. Another big one is ignoring thresholds; even a small lip can cause someone to stumble or make the walker catch. People also frequently focus only on the width and forget about the door hardware – is it easy to open with one hand, especially if grip strength is an issue? Lever handles are often much better than knobs. Lastly, don't just look at the doorway in isolation. Consider the entire approach path: are there tight hallway turns or obstructions that will force the walker user to twist or maneuver awkwardly? HH Remodeling LLC, serving the entire Denver Metro area, can help you avoid these pitfalls. We specialize in accessibility modifications and can provide a professional assessment to ensure your doorways truly support safe and independent movement. Give us a call if you need a hand!",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
